## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Multilingual Self</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> The Benefits of Multilingualism</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write reflective essays on personal experiences. Interactive: Write a group text on various topics, collaboratively, to be shared in a digital format.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a reflective essay about personal language learning experiences, highlighting the benefits of multilingualism (e.g., "When I started learning Guna, I felt shy to speak during family gatherings. Over time, practicing with my grandparents helped me feel more connected to my culture and community.")<br>Can collaboratively write a group text about the importance of language to be shared in a digital format (e.g., "Our group believes being bilingual helps connect with more people and can create better job opportunities. For example, in Panama, many employers prefer bilingual candidates.")<br>Can contribute to a shared text discussing cultural diversity (e.g., "Speaking multiple languages allows us to understand each other's cultures better. When I visited the San Blas Islands, I was able to greet locals in their language, which made the experience more meaningful.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276; width: 57%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key concepts and language for the writing task.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in a "Quick-Write" activity. The teacher displays a prompt: "Write for 3 minutes about how learning another <b>language</b> has (or could) expand your <b>opportunities</b> or understanding of <b>multicultural</b> communities in Panama." Students write continuously without stopping, focusing on generating ideas related to the theme "The Benefits of Multilingualism" and the scenario "My Multilingual Self." Encourage use of words like <i>bilingual, communication, skill</i>.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to start a reflective sentence using a modal of ability or reported speech, for example: "My friend <b>said that</b> being <b>fluent</b> in two languages <b>can</b> truly enhance your <b>career</b> prospects." The teacher writes this sentence on the board, pointing out the grammar and vocabulary. The teacher also explicitly models the pronunciation of /s/ in 'success' and /z/ in 'citizens' by saying them clearly, then asking students to repeat. The teacher then models word stress in 'multilingualism,' 'multicultural,' and 'language learning.'</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main idea we are writing about today?" (Expected answer: "The benefits of knowing many languages.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'reflective essay,' what kind of experiences should you write about?" (Expected answer: "Your own personal experiences or thoughts.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to "My Multilingual Self."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students are presented with short excerpts from three different reflective essays written by young Panamanians discussing their experiences with multilingualism. These excerpts showcase reported speech (e.g., "One student <b>argued that</b> true <b>communication will become</b> easier if we learn diverse languages."), future simple (e.g., "In the future, I <b>believe</b> schools <b>will prioritize</b> early language <b>education</b>."), and modals of ability (e.g., "You <b>can</b> really <b>enhance</b> your understanding of the world."). The excerpts include vocabulary like <i>proficiency, business, effective, diverse</i>.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student writes clear, coherent texts on a variety of subjects within their field of interest, synthesizing information and arguments from a number of sources.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the excerpts and identify the main benefit of multilingualism mentioned in each one, writing a brief summary sentence for each.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students underline or highlight all instances of reported speech, future simple, and modals of ability they find in the texts, then identify 5 target vocabulary words and write a synonym for each.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing, incorporating target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Developing written accuracy using reported speech, future simple, and modals of ability. Reinforcing vocabulary: <i>multilingualism, language, proficiency, communication, career, opportunities, business, education, fluency, skill, said, argued, believe, prioritize, offer, communicate, become, excel, enhance, learn, bilingual, multilingual, proficient, effective, international, diverse, multicultural, necessary, fluent</i>. Focusing on expressing opinions and predictions about multilingualism.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Sentence Transformation):</b> Students complete sentences by transforming direct quotes into reported speech, using appropriate reporting verbs. E.g., "My mother said, 'Being <b>bilingual</b> opens many doors.'" becomes "My mother <b>said that</b> being <b>bilingual</b> opened many doors." Or, "A classmate: 'I <b>believe</b> <b>multilingualism</b> is a vital <b>skill</b>.'" becomes "A classmate <b>believed that multilingualism</b> was a vital <b>skill</b>." Students complete 5-7 such sentences, ensuring correct tense and pronoun changes.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Paragraph Building):</b> Students are given sentence starters and a list of target vocabulary/grammar points (e.g., "future simple," "opportunities," "excel," "multicultural"). They must write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) about how multilingualism will impact a specific area (e.g., <b>business</b>, <b>education</b>, <b>international communication</b>), using future simple and at least three target vocabulary words. E.g., "Looking ahead, I predict that..." or "Students who <b>become fluent</b> in multiple languages..."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Collaborative Writing & Peer Review):</b> In pairs, students collectively write three sentences, each demonstrating a different grammatical structure: one with reported speech about language <b>proficiency</b>, one with future simple predicting job <b>opportunities</b>, and one with a modal of ability to describe how one <b>can enhance</b> their <b>communication skills</b>. They then exchange sentences with another pair, checking for grammatical accuracy, appropriate vocabulary usage, and clarity, providing constructive written feedback.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Multilingual Self."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reflective Essay Writing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4) to collaboratively write a short reflective essay (150-200 words) titled "Our Multilingual Journey: Connecting Cultures, Creating Futures." The essay should express their personal experiences and opinions about multilingualism, integrating the benefits learned. They must use at least two instances of reported speech, two instances of future simple, and two modals of ability. The essay should also include a minimum of five of the target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>multilingualism, opportunities, communication, skill, diverse, proficient</i>). Each group will share their essay in a digital format (e.g., a shared document, a class blog post).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b> The teacher circulates, providing support and feedback on vocabulary choice, grammatical structure, and overall coherence. The teacher encourages groups to discuss how "Multilingualism allows us to connect with people from different cultures and shows respect for their background" and to consider how "If we all learn even a little of each other's languages, we can make our community more inclusive and understanding" in their writing, reflecting the pragmatic functions. The teacher also reminds students to offer practical, culturally sensitive advice within their essays.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of student writing and self-reflection.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher-led rubric assessment and peer feedback.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups submit their collaboratively written reflective essays. Students also engage in a peer review process where each group reads another group's essay and provides written feedback using a simple checklist focusing on the criteria below.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Achievement:</b> Does the essay address the prompt effectively and highlight benefits of multilingualism? (0-2 points)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct use of reported speech, future simple, and modals of ability. (0-2 points)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Range and Accuracy:</b> Effective use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>multilingualism, opportunities, communication, skill</i>). (0-2 points)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Cohesion and Coherence:</b> Ideas are well-organized and flow logically. (0-2 points)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Evidence of group effort and contribution to the final text. (0-2 points)</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new vocabulary word or grammar structure I confidently used in my writing today was..."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The most challenging part of writing a collaborative essay was... and how we overcame it."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflection on learning and preparation for the next lesson.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did writing about multilingualism affect your understanding of its benefits in your daily life or for your future?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific strategies did your group use to ensure your collaborative writing was coherent and correctly used the target grammar?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The collaborative writing task worked well, promoting genuine interaction and negotiation of meaning. Students were able to incorporate most of the target grammar and vocabulary, particularly reported speech. Some groups struggled with maintaining consistent paragraph structure and ensuring smooth transitions between sentences. I noticed that the pragmatic advice about cultural inclusiveness was sometimes included generally but lacked specific examples."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"For the next lesson, I will provide a clear rubric for paragraph organization and transitions during the mediation task. I also need to provide more explicit examples of how to embed culturally sensitive advice with concrete scenarios relevant to Panama to deepen their pragmatic use. Additional scaffolding for sentence combining will be beneficial."</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson's collaborative reflective essays will serve as source material for the Mediation & Integration lesson (Lesson 5), where students will summarize and present key ideas from their own essays and those of their classmates in a different format, synthesizing the theme's core message as part of the 21st Century Skills project.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
</table>